Democratic Republic of Congo's Army has  been fighting the m-23 armed group up  and down this stretch of road for weeks  a Congolese Army Captain took us to the  front line it's near the border with  Rwanda which is widely understood to  back the armed group although Rwanda  denies it  this Soldier says we'll fight them back  to Rwanda whatever support they have  this is one of three front lines where  Congo's Army has fought M23 in recent  weeks this time a year ago M23 only had  a few hundred unarmed Fighters since  then the un's filmed Fighters entering  Congo from Rwanda and Rwandan soldiers  have been photographed among m23s  Fighters the soldiers here say they're  fighting an enemy that's better equipped  than them they say m23s Fighters get  more food more water and that they have  Heavy Artillery hidden on the hilltops  that makes it difficult for them to  fight and Advance  the captain told us their enemies on a  hillside less than a kilometer away M23  has rejected international calls for it  to withdraw and disarm by a deadline  that passed on Friday  Congo's Army says Rwanda wants military  control of this area through its proxy  M23  it's rich with golden coltan mines  Congo's government says M23 can't be  part of the Nairobi peace talks unless  it withdraws and disarms  we don't have time to waste because M23  and Rwanda only understand the voice of  bullets if they don't listen to the  calls of the leaders we'll have to force  them back to the place where they came  from  Congo's military has jet fighters and  new Attack Helicopters military sources  say they're wondering why the government  rarely orders them to bomb m23's  artillery and help the Infantry advance  nearly 300 000 people have fled the  fighting and are living in insanitary  camps many of them also question why the  government's not doing more  our soldiers are fighting with the  rebels but whenever they're about to win  the authorities tell them to stop  fighting for one or two days this is  exactly when the rebels always take  advantage and Advance we were told to  leave the area so that soldiers can  continue to fight  back at the front line soldiers told us  their enemy is used a few days of calm  to bring reinforcements from Rwanda  there's no sign of this conflict ending  soon Malcolm Webb Al Jazeera kibumba  Democratic Republic of Congo
 

